Joel Furr <jfurr@acpub.duke.edu> writes:
> There's been so much interest in the concept of perl/RSA t-shirts
> that more than one person is now making them.  *I* took orders until
> June 1 and accumulated about 1,500 orders, and have started taking
> orders for a second batch, with the ordering deadline of September
> 16.

Cool, glad to see such gleeful ITAR bashing, which is the point of the
exercise.  Impressive number of orders: 1500!, I've only got ~ 250 in
the UK from European folks so far (plus a few foreign nationals living
in the US, who can't be sold the shirts by a distributer in the US due
to ITAR), but then perhaps ITAR is not so well known in the EU.  Don I
think has shipped 500 now also, so that's a lot of people walking
around with munitions T's.  

There may in the future be a French shirt, in honour of their own
crypto regulations - worse even than ITAR in the US.  The French regs
are bad -- complete ban, permission required to own or use crypto
technically required from French government -- which they don't hand
out readily.  In fact the French regs are so bad that it may even be
necessary to produce them outside France to avoid technical legal
problems for the producer should the relevant govt agency decide to
get picky.

> My shirts do *not* include a markup for profit -- they are sold at a
> break-even price of $12.36.

Mine are likewise (sold at an estimated break even price, excess due
to better price obtained from larger than expected order goes to Phil
Z also), Lucy Chubbs (Australia) are break even also, Don Hensons are
commercial with 25% of profits to Phil Z, and I see no problem with
that.

(A small quibble, also is that you on your netstuff pages, do ask for
a donation to help cover your time, which is fine by me, and you're
welcome to it, T-shirt tinkering certainly eats time, but some people
will be honouring this request, and hence feel bound out of politeness
to pay you extra over your quoted $12.36, so perhaps you are not quite
comparing like with like - Don = 75% + 25% to Phil Z, Joel = 0% + x%
donations.  Note that I'm *not* compaining, just defending Don, who
has been helpful in setting up more T-shirt orders).

> A guy named Don Henson, somewhere in Texas, is also doing similar
> shirts, and is charging $15.95 -- $3.50 more than I am.  

Mitigated by some other facts:

a) Don is a PGP user, and crypto enthousiast,libertarian and privacy advocate
b) provided a useful service to people who couldn't get your shirts when 
   the 1st order ran out, he's still shipping - shipped 2nd batch already
c) is donating 25% to the Phil Z legal defense fund - with Phil Zs
   blessing, 
d) asked permission first (asked me, Phil Zimmermann, and Josh Osborne),and
e) is always polite and courteous, 
f) provided a very prompt delivery to those who ordered through him,
g) takes credit card orders (encrypted with his PGP key for your saftey
   if you use PGP), also take Netcash,
h) doesn't require notary signatures

So yes your's are cheaper, however,... credit card dealerships cost
money, but are convenient to use until ecash gets here for real
(comeon Digicash *where are you*), his prompt service costs him time,
and he doesn't require a notary signature (I think they are, or can be
obtained for free if you shop around?, but there is still
inconvenience), so his system of payment is entirely on-line, and very
secure if the purchaser cares to use PGP.

> However, he's also making weird claims like "it's illegal to wear
> one of these t-shirts in front of someone who's not an American
> citizen."  That's not true.  In fact, I don't know where he got that
> idea.  

No, it is true.  Well actually, it is not so black and white, it is
perhaps debatable (it was debated ad nauseum on cypherpunks list a
while ago until everyone got sick of the subject), but there is a
section of the ITAR regulations (what the whole shirt is in honour of
right?) which says otherwise, this little gem:

Here, verbatim is section 120.17(4) of the ITAR regulations:

> (4)  Disclosing (including oral or visual disclosure) or transferring
>      technical data to a foreign person, whether in the United States
>      or abroad

So when you consider that the T-shirt is the technical data, and the
proud wearer is doing the disclosing you can see where the fun starts,
and where Don gets his interpretation from.

Disclosing ... visually ... technical data to a foreign person ... in
the United States.

Sound like a T-shirt? visually? sounds good, to a foreign person,
plenty of foreign nationals in the US, in your class, your neibour,
your fellow students, researchers, (heck Adi Shamir the S from RSA is
a foreign national, leading to amazingly ridiculous conclusions - he
co-developed it - were his US colleages breaking the law by
collaborating with him?)

USC 2778 & 2779 say that the penalties for violating ITAR are
$1,000,000 and/or up to 10 years imprisonment per count of violation.

Full text of ITAR is in John Gilmores ITAR & export collection:

	ftp://ftp.cygnus.com/pub/export/itar.in.full

> Yes, it's illegal to sell strong encryption code in machine readable
> form to anyone who is not an American citizen, Canadian citizen, or
> American permanent resident, but it's sure as hell not illegal to
> walk past someone wearing a shirt with perl/RSA code on the shirt in
> a machine-readable font.

Well, it is certainly ridiculous, granted, but strict reading of the
ITARs seem to say just this.

There is much about ITAR which is contested, many (even a goverment
hearing of the OLC) think that the ITAR regulations are
unconstitutional, and illegal, but the NSA is still grimly holding on
to their ability to perform illegal wire-taps.  The EFF is fighting an
ITAR related case on constitutional grounds at the moment, the Dan
Bernstein case.  EPIC has extracted documents from the FBI and the NSA
with FOIA requests which show that even some FBI and NSA lawyers
believe that they would have a poor case if ITAR gets to the courts
with a un-constitutional claim.

You will note that there is still a huge government push on for
escrowed-crypto (read government access to keys - GAK).

This is the big enemy, and what folks should be fighting IMO.

> If people are interested in getting ordering information for the
> *original* perl/RSA t-shirts, ordering information is available at
> http://www.danger.com/netstuff.html or by emailing me directly. 

As far as I'm concerned people are welcome to get their shirts from
you, or anyone else who wishes to print them.  Presumably you have no
objections as the more other suppliers there are, the less (unpaid)
work for you?

There are currently 4 suppliers of T-shirts:

EU - me printed in the UK, will ship to anywhere (no ITAR considerations)
AU - Lucy Chubb <lucyc@suite.sw.oz.au> likewise anywhere
US - Joel Furr US & Canada only, notary public signature required
US - Don Henson US & Canada only, credit cards accepted, no notary required

I wrote the code, and Josh Osborne asked you to do the shirt.  The
whole aim is to get as many shirts as possible out there, and take the
mick out of the incredibly silly US ITAR regulations, which are costing
the US software industry estimated 100s of millions of dollars in lost
trade to EU and other competitors who don't have to contend with ITAR.
I personally don't see anything grossly unfair, or unethical about Don
Henson making some profit for his time, he did produce a very fast
turn-around, and has shipped his 2nd batch before you've even shipped
the first in spite of the fact that you stopped accepting orders
around one month before he started accepting orders.  

I'm not complaining, you've been extremely helpful in setting up the
thing for the 1st batch at Josh Osbornes request, and you have (or
soon will have) personally shipped more munitions shirts than anyone
else put together - that's the A1 priority for me - bad publicity for
ITAR, lots of shirts sold.  Also I couldn't very well complain of your
turn around time, mine is likely to turn out similarly slow, I've just
ordered my 1st batch of 250, and people were begining to ask
(politely) about delivery times.  So I can entirely sympathise with
having other things to do, and not doing it for a job, etc.  Also 1500
shirts is a lot of shirts, and a lot of orders to deal with!

My collection of people shipping shirts needs updating to include you
again, I'll do that, all the details on the perl-rsa sig, and the
legal background is on:

	http://dcs.ex.ac.uk/~aba/rsa/

It would be helpful if you put a pointer to that on your netstuff page
for the perl/RSA offer, to ensure that people get the background
political information which goes with the shirt, which is the whole
aim with the munitions sig, and T-shirt, tatoo (yes really two people
in the US are arranging to become a bio-munition), bumper sticker,
badge, etc., etc.

